[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543531 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fb557ae-9a47-526e-ae26-b4f9d80275d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542726} {:answer/id #uuid "a31007c0-bb7e-5195-b1fe-bac89c68dbbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543070} {:answer/id #uuid "cff918bc-b5d0-5906-8d66-bbe4168f3474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543178} {:answer/id #uuid "daad00e3-599f-5edb-abd8-30682e338221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543199} {:answer/id #uuid "de2a4dff-046b-5136-b0e7-22b11eceaca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a31007c0-bb7e-5195-b1fe-bac89c68dbbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543070} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aab8c1f-76ff-5a2c-9c46-d7294b8c857e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9796dfce-fb6b-57bd-b442-e9278daaaa20" :question/math-academy-id "q-98925" :question/problem "If ${log}_{2}⁡(x)=k$ and ${log}_{2}⁡(y)=4$, then ${log}_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that ${log}_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3})$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3}) & ={log}_{2}⁡(x)+{log}_{2}⁡({y}^{3}) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(x)+3{log}_{2}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${log}_{2}⁡(x)=k$ and ${log}_{2}⁡(y)=4,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+3{log}_{2}⁡(y) & =k+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =k+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3})=k+12.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ab9f307-2949-509c-aa21-cb7f2f79269e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{p}{q}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542798} {:answer/id #uuid "475b221e-4c3d-508a-8794-b0910728ee87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p-q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542833} {:answer/id #uuid "5a7458a8-57c1-50ff-a7eb-323cf41e0146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542886} {:answer/id #uuid "8fadf0d1-ce58-5388-bbca-029241c7ef65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{q}{p}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543014} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e5be19-84e6-54b6-a352-1c0c4517f128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "475b221e-4c3d-508a-8794-b0910728ee87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p-q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542833} :answer-field/id #uuid "2842ede1-7316-5852-8316-724915e58cee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99f3a3dc-a29b-56b3-b6b2-eb00c09e4e97" :question/math-academy-id "q-24442" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)=p$ and $\\operatorname{log}⁡(y)=q$, then $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{y})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the quotient rule for common logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(b)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for common logarithms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{y}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(y) \\\\  & =p-q\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543541 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1de3d2cd-60aa-59f4-9c1c-0d3d70e7064b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x}-{y}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542718} {:answer/id #uuid "3adf2bc5-273b-5e81-9d24-ceeca3bf0302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{5}⁡(\\sqrt{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542800} {:answer/id #uuid "6a2fdb68-0b95-5c0e-94b2-81028526e2ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{5y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542927} {:answer/id #uuid "896cc279-0ed5-5375-8db2-e1b363fec667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543003} {:answer/id #uuid "b7267a79-3839-5f21-b62c-09c9545b4060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{5}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7267a79-3839-5f21-b62c-09c9545b4060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{5}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543117} :answer-field/id #uuid "3df2d483-a123-580b-8ed7-a2a425df48f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8ef19a1-f2fa-5f29-a4ae-a1f3f6bb227d" :question/math-academy-id "q-1110" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(x)-5{log}_{5}⁡(y)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the power rule and then the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(x)-5{log}_{5}⁡(y) & ={log}_{5}⁡({x}^{1/2})-{log}_{5}⁡({y}^{5}) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡(\\sqrt{x})-{log}_{5}⁡({y}^{5}) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{5}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543544 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47698303-8d61-5840-8827-86edae5c0ca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542834} {:answer/id #uuid "48989a49-93e8-5124-b600-ec90d999d72c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542838} {:answer/id #uuid "7eef2b9f-e7ca-51a3-b197-a936d17dd502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542981} {:answer/id #uuid "cc28b179-5541-543f-9559-8ecc8381e121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543171} {:answer/id #uuid "e5647be5-c113-5d6c-87be-376b749ccd3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eef2b9f-e7ca-51a3-b197-a936d17dd502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542981} :answer-field/id #uuid "2253fa4d-3bff-5c5d-817e-be26b444097e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aaf34c24-70f2-59f0-ad3e-3eb728abaa5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-159961" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4},{X}_{5}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,{1}^{2}),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. The log-likelihood function of the sample $0.7,\\,0.6,\\,-0.5,\\,1.7,\\,-1.1$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-2.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+a+b\\theta +c{\\theta }^{2}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{x-\\theta }{\\sigma })}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{(x-\\theta )^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0.7+0.6+(-0.5)+1.7+(-1.1) \\\\  & =1.4 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.7)^{2}+(0.6)^{2}+(-0.5)^{2}+(1.7)^{2}+(-1.1)^{2} \\\\  & =5.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=5.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(5.2-2\\theta \\cdot (1.4)+5{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-2.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+(-2.6)+(1.4)\\theta +(-2.5){\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=-2.6+(1.4)+(-2.5)=-3.7.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06386582-1858-5b59-a352-479a8fbec1ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k-m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542651} {:answer/id #uuid "a7595e68-63e0-55b4-9190-55fd9ad8ee38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k+m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543080} {:answer/id #uuid "c8679008-38bf-5c0c-91af-6a3638018d35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{k}{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543162} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e08e8d-ec38-59a8-94ea-46959cbfc389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "km" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543225} {:answer/id #uuid "ed27e2cf-d889-5413-8f07-fe2a732afd99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "m-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06386582-1858-5b59-a352-479a8fbec1ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k-m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542651} :answer-field/id #uuid "54df9ca0-9526-59ba-8d0c-624262e20b11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ada9c1dd-522d-5138-9cfe-44ee7af19872" :question/math-academy-id "q-108201" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{log}⁡(y)=k$ and $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})=m$, then $\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{y}{(\\frac{y}{x})}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(y)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =k-m.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543549 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "312ee280-346c-51be-9a5b-40f3ad5f100e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(7t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542770} {:answer/id #uuid "5543e5e9-fa34-55a2-963c-46317805891c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{7}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542869} {:answer/id #uuid "61eaa231-d53f-5412-a297-547a805089db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{7t}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542907} {:answer/id #uuid "ce45adcc-4013-5eaa-828b-6c4c5f9aefd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(t+7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543175} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a71173-deae-5155-8154-2b2bf6b3687d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡({t}^{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7a71173-deae-5155-8154-2b2bf6b3687d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡({t}^{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543275} :answer-field/id #uuid "eba2b04a-58b1-5797-a2dc-057b2fcfd9dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b071d322-d430-5f91-b157-0d44ee6fac29" :question/math-academy-id "q-100019" :question/problem "$7{log}_{2}⁡t=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡({c}^{m})=m\\operatorname{log}⁡(c)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n7{log}_{2}⁡t={log}_{2}⁡({t}^{7}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07fd8dbf-8464-5213-809c-abf7fe832f06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542654} {:answer/id #uuid "3c779408-7831-5160-b0c8-60656efd484a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542806} {:answer/id #uuid "9a82f221-b74d-53a8-8503-dd83640fa112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543045} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b697a9-1b21-56af-8f8c-9f4c93fcecf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543073} {:answer/id #uuid "ac2ba49b-f75f-5551-9a43-d83d73aeffba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a82f221-b74d-53a8-8503-dd83640fa112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543045} :answer-field/id #uuid "949b8458-1c9d-5893-a395-0d0724748af6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b69fb899-dda9-520e-9df1-5f655ac32f35" :question/math-academy-id "q-121354" :question/problem "The polar curve $r=k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ where $k$ is a constant, passes through the point $A$ with Cartesian coordinates $(-1,\\sqrt{3}).$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the polar coordinates of the point $A.$ The distance from $A$ to the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A$ lies in the second quadrant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $A$ are $(r,\\theta )=(2,\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).$ \n\nTo find the value of $k$, we substitute the polar coordinates of the point $A$ into the polar equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2 & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\ 2 & =k(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ k & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is $r=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f85ddaa01b6f2232627375ec622232a245c6e7e21fb9e294cd0801fd45433d73.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543556 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24d2769f-5f35-5619-a06a-6da4d0cd9d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24{\\pi }^{2}}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542734} {:answer/id #uuid "3adfe199-8aa8-54b5-af09-00ff18169df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11{\\pi }^{2}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542801} {:answer/id #uuid "66f3e329-d6ad-5cda-b6ea-849f556393e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8{\\pi }^{2}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542916} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd4b999-29de-5078-b62a-6736401df8ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17{\\pi }^{2}}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543052} {:answer/id #uuid "b72530e6-e6de-50bd-a6e2-1633d11cef2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11{\\pi }^{2}}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cd4b999-29de-5078-b62a-6736401df8ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17{\\pi }^{2}}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543052} :answer-field/id #uuid "babdd4ab-5b3d-5b5f-8892-4e5632d1283f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c08c7d82-b9b5-59ff-a4f6-2f1eac5d7e57" :question/math-academy-id "q-121537" :question/problem "Consider the equation $2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2})+3=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{5}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2})+3 & =0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}) & =-3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}) & =-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x}{2} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & \\frac{5x}{2} & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}+4n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 5x & =\\frac{7\\pi }{3}+4n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{4n\\pi }{5} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{7\\pi }{15}+\\frac{4n\\pi }{5} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(12n+5)\\pi }{15} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(12n+7)\\pi }{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{15}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{17\\pi }{15}$  and $x=\\frac{19\\pi }{15}.$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{29\\pi }{15}$  and $x=\\frac{31\\pi }{15}.$ We reject the solution $\\frac{31\\pi }{15}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},{x}_{5}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{15},\\,\\frac{17\\pi }{15},\\,\\frac{19\\pi }{15},\\,\\frac{29\\pi }{15}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{17\\pi }{15}=\\frac{17{\\pi }^{2}}{45}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543573 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10851533-4282-5b80-bae7-98d8fc92685d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{log}_{3}⁡(x)+3{log}_{3}⁡(y)-\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542678} {:answer/id #uuid "290b87bb-8570-5cb4-83b9-b6d2e59941cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{log}_{3}⁡(x)+3{log}_{3}⁡(y)+\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542749} {:answer/id #uuid "599a54dc-6c33-56a6-8830-1ae45124f78d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{3}⁡(x)-{log}_{3}⁡(y)-\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542884} {:answer/id #uuid "84635862-dc5f-5a84-88a5-ebc9a5b22bda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{log}_{3}⁡(x)+{log}_{3}⁡(y)-\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542994} {:answer/id #uuid "91fec59e-a5fa-5664-9d8b-2381d008fe09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{3}⁡(x)+{log}_{3}⁡(y)+\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10851533-4282-5b80-bae7-98d8fc92685d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{log}_{3}⁡(x)+3{log}_{3}⁡(y)-\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542678} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ccb3dbb-60a9-5fb3-a5b6-44c5f5ad437b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3b649a8-d282-53b7-b8c8-e883fd47c0b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-79406" :question/problem "${log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{\\sqrt{z}})=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{\\sqrt{z}})={log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})-{log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{z})\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})-{log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{z})={log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})+{log}_{3}⁡({y}^{3})-{log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{z}).\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})+{log}_{3}⁡({y}^{3})-{log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{z})=2{log}_{3}⁡(x)+3{log}_{3}⁡(y)-\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(z).\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{\\sqrt{z}})=2{log}_{3}⁡(x)+3{log}_{3}⁡(y)-\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(z).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c75f4c4b-c7a0-59c5-8797-cff98d56b03c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c75f4c4b-c7a0-59c5-8797-cff98d56b03c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543159} :answer-field/id #uuid "a12b2c7e-438f-5baa-bede-06e74205a643" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6d790e0-5c33-5dec-949d-78146efef13a" :question/math-academy-id "q-250840" :question/problem "Given that $\\frac{x}{4}$, $x$, and $64$ are the first three terms of a geometric sequence, find $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The first three terms are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=\\frac{x}{4},\\,{a}_{2}=x,\\,{a}_{3}=64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio of the first two terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{x}{(\\frac{x}{4})}=x\\cdot \\frac{4}{x}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the common ratio of the second and third terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{64}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the values for $r$ and solving for $x,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =\\frac{64}{x} \\\\ 4x & =64 \\\\ x & =\\frac{64}{4} \\\\ x & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543577 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e893e4b-1f92-52ad-ad87-c504cbe090a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{4}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x}}{y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542671} {:answer/id #uuid "57aa5928-9fe8-571f-bec1-84a8858c534f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{4}⁡(\\frac{3x}{y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542879} {:answer/id #uuid "5db0ca0e-d6da-543d-94a6-0b0616316cbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{4}⁡(\\frac{x}{3y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542891} {:answer/id #uuid "c76a97fc-fdf4-5ad6-8243-95edbdc9a876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{4}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{x}y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543160} {:answer/id #uuid "ecfa35ae-3197-55c3-8180-1535e0873fbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{4}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e893e4b-1f92-52ad-ad87-c504cbe090a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{4}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x}}{y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542671} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb4f622f-2866-535c-a540-b48bd6d1ad52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e70f2288-3b25-5d12-a8c5-361a6e66e0f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-100243" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{1}{3}{log}_{4}⁡(x)-{log}_{4}⁡(y)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the power rule and then the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{log}_{4}⁡(x)-{log}_{4}⁡(y) & ={log}_{4}⁡({x}^{1/3})-{log}_{4}⁡(y) \\\\  & ={log}_{4}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{x})-{log}_{4}⁡(y) \\\\  & ={log}_{4}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x}}{y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543812 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "576b4a58-c183-5275-bda2-05fe1ba9c5fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{59}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543815} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff2ff02-02b1-5751-b12a-7e9ef64a27e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{97}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543820} {:answer/id #uuid "857a4020-99d9-5010-bea5-959744696366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{59}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543821} {:answer/id #uuid "a3810977-df2d-5104-b3e0-f2a4d0401184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543827} {:answer/id #uuid "f91c3520-3e82-5867-91f7-0be0499e3c49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "857a4020-99d9-5010-bea5-959744696366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{59}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543821} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ab8a066-f63f-5bc4-a451-8cc061d5437d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2a13fd10-2736-5163-8e91-3a139268a2d1" :question/problem "![](images/5e/5e11606627f4b91c977670cf9f919aff8b659cb5b4776cf3d04bb561b50b79fb.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=- \\frac{17}{2},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=- \\frac{19}{3},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{23}{3},\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1},$ ${C}_{2},$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/fa/fa1022d2a1718627898289abdd4d3035a6e06230eea16d0e46832675b2e37def.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientation of the outer curve ${C}_{1}$ is opposite that of ${C}_{1}$ as stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-(- \\frac{17}{2})+(- \\frac{19}{3})+\\frac{23}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-51)+(-38)+46}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{59}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543825 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02fb5c25-2a44-5658-8d3a-94fee2cfc5f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543806} {:answer/id #uuid "08456781-dc15-5446-8a0d-a30462b07db6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{37}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543809} {:answer/id #uuid "5aa28b88-1b4c-5f38-a927-2f0a3b55f78c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543817} {:answer/id #uuid "73ed4b27-596d-5370-95af-6d3edadf7860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543818} {:answer/id #uuid "950d0158-2f90-5533-a993-2f44cea84fca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{58}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5aa28b88-1b4c-5f38-a927-2f0a3b55f78c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543817} :answer-field/id #uuid "10013332-1268-5bf0-abb7-b697ec1223bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9be43ff2-1cbd-5518-ad47-108fe13ce12b" :question/problem "![](images/5e/5e11606627f4b91c977670cf9f919aff8b659cb5b4776cf3d04bb561b50b79fb.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{19}{3},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=- \\frac{7}{2},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{19}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1},$ ${C}_{2},$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/fa/fa1022d2a1718627898289abdd4d3035a6e06230eea16d0e46832675b2e37def.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientation of the outer curve ${C}_{1}$ is opposite that of ${C}_{1}$ as stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-\\frac{19}{3}+(- \\frac{7}{2})+\\frac{19}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-38+(-21)+57}{6} \\\\  & =- \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "545c9291-08a2-5911-a792-6f15761476b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{121}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543814} {:answer/id #uuid "7a00a83b-01e3-57a2-a8d3-f075c520f92d" :answer/type {:db/